                                    <h1>Telemedicine Platform Development: Complete Guide 2026</h1>
                                    <p>Telemedicine went from experimental to standard-of-care during COVID and has now settled into mature product categories: primary care consult, specialist consult, chronic disease management, mental health, and hospital-at-home. Here is what a modern telemedicine platform looks like end-to-end.</p>
                                    <h2>The Telemedicine Platform Layers</h2>
                                    <ol><li><strong>Patient app / web</strong> — symptom intake, doctor discovery, booking, consult, follow-up.</li><li><strong>Doctor app / web</strong> — schedule, patient list, consult UI, notes, prescription writing.</li><li><strong>Video infrastructure</strong> — Twilio / 100ms / Vonage / LiveKit.</li><li><strong>EMR / EHR</strong> — patient records, notes, history.</li><li><strong>E-prescription</strong> — digital Rx with signature, integration with pharmacies.</li><li><strong>E-pharmacy integration</strong> — order fulfillment.</li><li><strong>Lab integration</strong> — test order, results retrieval.</li><li><strong>Payment + insurance</strong> — patient billing, TPA claim submission.</li><li><strong>Admin ops console</strong> — doctor onboarding, quality management.</li></ol>
                                    <h2>Regulatory Requirements</h2>
                                    <p><strong>India:</strong> Telemedicine Practice Guidelines 2020 (MoHFW), DPDP Act, doctor registration via NMC.<br><strong>UAE:</strong> DHA (Dubai) / DOH (Abu Dhabi) telehealth guidelines, ADHICS data security, PDPL.<br><strong>USA:</strong> HIPAA (mandatory), state-by-state telemedicine rules, DEA for controlled substance prescription.<br><strong>UK:</strong> CQC registration, DSPT, MHRA if any diagnostic claim.<br><strong>Australia:</strong> TGA regulations, Privacy Act, state health department guidelines.</p>
                                    <h2>Video Infrastructure Choice</h2>
                                    <p>Twilio Video: proven, expensive at scale. 100ms: India-first, competitive pricing, strong quality. Vonage / OpenTok: legacy but reliable. LiveKit: open-source, self-hostable. WebRTC direct: only for very-high-scale where you want to eliminate vendor. For most: Twilio or 100ms.</p>
                                    <h2>Cost + Timeline</h2>
                                    <p><strong>MVP telemedicine (16-22 weeks):</strong> ₹40-80 lakh / $48K-96K.<br><strong>Growth platform (22-36 weeks):</strong> ₹1-2.5 Cr / $120K-300K.<br><strong>Enterprise hospital-integrated telemedicine (36-52 weeks):</strong> ₹2.5-6 Cr / $300K-720K.<br>Video infrastructure ongoing: $0.005-0.02/minute per participant.</p>
                                    <h2>The AI Layer That Matters</h2>
                                    <p>Symptom triage before consult (routes patient to right specialist). Ambient consult documentation (saves doctor 60-90 min/day). Prescription drug interaction checking. Follow-up reminders + adherence tracking. Well-implemented AI cuts consult time 15-30% and lifts patient satisfaction.</p>

